Research Engineer
United Kingdom

&nbsp;

Grade UE07: £41,885 – £49,798 per annum, pro-rata is part-time

College of Science and Engineering&nbsp;/ School of Informatics

Full-time: 35 hours per week&nbsp;

Fixed-term: 6 months&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Together we can do great things. Be part of something bigger.&nbsp;

With roles from hospitality to research, there’s a career for everyone at the University of Edinburgh.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

The Opportunity:
&nbsp;

The School of Informatics is looking for a Research Engineer to join the 

Blockchain Technology Laboratory

 and build software that lets AI agents find, trust and pay one another.&nbsp;

The system lets each AI agent publish an on-chain record of what it does, where it can be reached and what it charges, so that other agents can use that record to find it and pay per request on the 

Midnight

 blockchain. With support from the Principal Investigator, you will develop it, from smart contracts on Midnight to agent services that use the 

Agent2Agent (A2A) protocol

 and per-request payments (for example, following the 

x402 standard

). The Principal Investigator provides a high-level specification of what the system must do, and the implementation approach and architecture are up to you: you may build on the proposed 

Midnight Agent Identity Standard (MIP-0029)

, extend it or take a different approach.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Your skills and attributes for success:
&nbsp;

Building software that works with a blockchain, such as smart contracts, wallets or client libraries.&nbsp;

Writing services and HTTP APIs in TypeScript, JavaScript or another modern programming language.&nbsp;

Proven professional software engineering experience, including delivering working software independently, from design to release.&nbsp;

Good software engineering habits, such as version control, automated testing and clear documentation.&nbsp;

Learning new languages and tools, such as Midnight’s 

Compact

&nbsp;
smart-contract language.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Experience of building AI agents, of HTTP payment protocols such as x402, or of Midnight would be an advantage, but is not essential.&nbsp;

The post is part of the research project “Trustworthy Agentic Blockchain Systems”, funded by Input Output, and sits in the 

Blockchain Technology Laboratory

, directed by Professor Aggelos Kiayias, Chair in Cyber Security and Privacy and Chief Scientist at Input Output. In the 2021 Research Excellence Framework, the University of Edinburgh’s Computer Science and Informatics submission, made jointly by the School of Informatics and EPCC (the University’s Supercomputing and Data Science Centre), was ranked first in the UK for research power by Times Higher Education (source: 

Times Higher Education, 12 May 2022

).&nbsp;

This post is full-time (35 hours per week); however, we are open to considering part-time or flexible working patterns. &nbsp;

