Postdoctoral Research Associate – Socio-Legal / Socio-Economic Research (REWIRE)
Edinburgh - Central Area, Midlothian, United Kingdom

&nbsp;

Grade UE07: £41,885 - £49,798 per annum pro-rata as part-time

CAHSS / Business School / ERC Starting Grant REWIRE

Part-time: 28 hours per week (0.80 FTE)

Fixed-term: 36 months; anticipated start 1
st
 March 2027

&nbsp;

The Opportunity:

Help shape the future of neuroinclusive workplaces through comparative European research.

Join the University of Edinburgh Business School and contribute to REWIRE, a five-year ERC Starting Grant investigating how legal recognition, organisational practices and worker experiences shape workplace inclusion for neurodivergent people across the UK, Germany, Sweden, Spain and Italy.

We seek an ambitious and collaborative Postdoctoral Research Associate to lead core socio-legal and socio-economic research in Work Package 1, working closely with the Principal Investigator and an interdisciplinary international team.

You will assemble and analyse a five-country corpus of employment tribunal and equality-body decisions; develop comparative coding frameworks, legal recognition maps and country reports; contribute to organisational vignettes and worker research; and produce high-quality journal publications. You will also assist with project management, milestone tracking, meetings and reporting, and may undertake limited teaching subject to workload allocation and ERC funding conditions. Applicants should hold a PhD in socio-legal studies, employment/labour law, sociology, socio-economics, Business, Management, HRM, or a related field (or have submitted their thesis, subject to HR policy). We seek a strong track record of peer-reviewed publications, including leading international journals relevant to the post, commensurate with career stage, and clear potential to publish in top-tier journals. Comparative qualitative research and rigorous data-management experience are essential.

We welcome applications from researchers committed to rigorous, collaborative and impactful scholarship. Please demonstrate how your experience meets the essential and desirable criteria in the job description.

The University offers comprehensive benefits, including annual leave, pension provision and flexible working opportunities, subject to policy.

This post is part-time (0.80 FTE). Requests for flexible or hybrid working arrangements may be considered in accordance with University policy and project needs.

